fine_print:
Only public auction sales count. The work must be a painting, and the amount must meet or exceed US\$100,000,000 as reported by the auction house. Private sales do not count.

If a single sale price is reported by the auction house, then that price will be used for resolution. If the auction house reports hammer prices and buyer's fees (explained [here](https://www.barnebys.com/blog/auctions-101-hammer-price-vs.-buyers-premium)) separately, then both will be summed for resolution.&#x20;

For purposes of this question, a “painting” is a work categorized by the auction house as a painting in its official sale listing, press release, or other public pronouncements.

At the [resolution link](https://epoch.ai/benchmarks/gpqa-diamond), the resolution numbers are at the Leaderboard tab.&#x20;

Please note that due to the importance of methodology and impartial third party evaluation, only scores presented by Epoch count for this question. For example, on November 18, 2025, Google published a blog post [announcing ](https://blog.google/products/gemini/gemini-3/#gemini-3-deep-think)a score of 93.8% by Gemini 3 Deep Think. However, at the time of this question that score was not included in Epoch's benchmarking Leaderboard, so it does not count.&#x20;

Additionally, in the event Accuracy numbers are presented as a range and a specific number is no longer given by Epoch, the midpoint will be used. At the time of this question, the highest accuracy score was 92.6%, achieved by Gemini 3 Pro Preview: <img height="226" width="757" src="https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_6.57.42PM.png" />

